  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What starts a cat purring?",
      "a": "Scientists have long thought a rhythm generator in the brain sends fast signals to the larynx muscles. A 2023 study found a cat's larynx can also make purr-like vibrations with airflow alone, so the full story is still being worked out.",
      "asks": [
        "Is purring controlled by the brain?",
        "Do cats purr on purpose?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do cat larynx muscles make a purr?",
      "a": "The larynx muscles twitch rapidly, about 25 to 150 times a second, so the vocal folds snap open and shut. Each snap makes a tiny pulse of sound, and together they make the steady rumble.",
      "asks": [
        "How fast does a cat purr?",
        "What part of a cat makes the purr?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is a purr so low?",
      "a": "Purring is a very low sound for such a small animal. Researchers found fleshy pads in cats' vocal folds that make them heavier, so they vibrate slowly and give the deep rumble.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the pads in cat vocal folds?",
        "Why does a purr sound deep?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can cats purr while breathing in?",
      "a": "Yes. Unlike a meow, which happens only as the cat breathes out, purring keeps going as air flows in and out, with just a tiny change in sound between the two.",
      "asks": [
        "How can a cat purr nonstop?",
        "Does a cat purr in and out?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do cats purr only when happy?",
      "a": "No. Cats purr when content, but also when nervous, hurt, giving birth or even dying. Kittens purr while feeding, and some cats add a cry-like note to a purr when they want food.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do cats purr when they are hurt?",
        "What does a cat's purr mean?"
      ]
    }
  ],
